==Sabaean==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|xsa|sem-wes-pro|*paraš-}}. Belongs to a widespread Semitic equestrian word-family. Cognate with {{cog|ar|فَرَس|t=horse, mare}} and {{cog|gez|ፈረስ|tr=färäs|t=horse}}. Compare Ḥaḍramitic {{m|xhd|𐩱𐩰𐩧𐩪|tr=ʾfrs¹|t=cavalry}}, Safaitic <span class="mention-tr tr Latn" lang="und">frs</span> “horse; horseman”, and Yemeni Arabic {{m|ar|فَرَس|t=mare}}.

The human sense belongs to the same equestrian family; compare {{m|ar|فَارِس|t=horseman, rider}} and Yemeni Arabic {{m|ar|فَارِس|t=rider}}. Mehri <span class="mention-tr tr Latn" lang="und">fərháyn</span> and Ḥarsusi <span class="mention-tr tr Latn" lang="und">ferhīn</span>, both “mare; horse”, provide further Modern South Arabian parallels, though with a different final radical.

The Sabaean plural stem {{m|xsa|𐩱𐩰𐩧𐩪|tr=ʾfrs¹}} closely resembles Arabic {{m|ar|أَفْرَاس|t=horses}}, the broken plural of {{m|ar|فَرَس}}.

===Noun===
{{xsa-noun|tr=frs¹m}}

# A [[horse]]; [[mare]].
#* {{RQ:DASI|title=Ja 745|url=https://dasi.cnr.it/csai-epi-6120|section=lines 6–10|passage=ḥmdm b-ḏt s¹twfy '''frs¹ mlkn''' Gdn b-kn rkb-hw ʿdy S¹hrtn w-ḥmdm b-ḏt mtʿ '''frs¹nhn''' Dynrm w-Ẓbym|translation=in praise because He protected the king's horse Gdn when he rode it to S¹hrtn, and in praise because He saved the two horses Dynrm and Ẓbym}}
# A representation of a horse, especially a [[horse]] [[statuette]] or other votive image.
#* {{RQ:DASI|title=Ja 666|url=https://dasi.cnr.it/csai-epi-6061|section=lines 3–5|passage=hqnyw mrʾ-hmw ʾlmqh bʿl ʾwm '''frs¹m w-rkb-hw kly ḏhbm''' ḏ-s²ft-hw|translation=they dedicated to their lord ʾlmqh, Lord of ʾwm, a horse and its rider, both in bronze, which they had promised Him}}
#* {{RQ:DASI|title=Ja 752|url=https://dasi.cnr.it/csai-epi-6127|section=lines 5–9|passage=hqnyw mrʾ-hmw ʾlmqhw bʿl ʾwm '''frs¹m ḏt ḏhbm''' ḏt s²ft-hw|translation=they dedicated to their lord ʾlmqhw, Lord of ʾwm, a horse in bronze which they had promised Him}}
# A [[horseman]], mounted [[rider]] or member of a cavalry force, especially in the plural.
#* {{RQ:DASI|title=Ja 665|url=https://dasi.cnr.it/csai-epi-6060|section=lines 15–17|passage=ʾttmn kl gys²-hmw ḫms¹y w-s¹bʿ mʾtm ʾs¹dm rkbm w-'''s¹bʿy ʾfrs¹m'''|translation=their entire contingent assembled and mustered, seven hundred and fifty soldiers mounted on camels and seventy horsemen}}

====Usage notes====
The distinction between a horse and a horseman is normally supplied by context. In military enumerations, plural {{m|xsa|𐩱𐩰𐩧𐩪𐩣|tr=ʾfrs¹m}} can count horsemen; in descriptions of booty or captured animals, the same plural refers to horses.

Dedicatory inscriptions provide a third characteristic context. A horse described as {{m|xsa|𐩹𐩩𐩽𐩹𐩠𐩨𐩣|tr=ḏt ḏhbm|t=of bronze}} is a represented horse or horse-shaped votive object rather than a living animal. Horse and rider can be dedicated together as a pair.

The construct and suffix-bearing singular is {{m|xsa|𐩰𐩧𐩪|tr=frs¹}}. The determinate singular is {{m|xsa|𐩰𐩧𐩪𐩬|tr=frs¹n}}. The plural has construct/suffix form {{m|xsa|𐩱𐩰𐩧𐩪|tr=ʾfrs¹}}, indeterminate {{m|xsa|𐩱𐩰𐩧𐩪𐩣|tr=ʾfrs¹m}}, and determinate {{m|xsa|𐩱𐩰𐩧𐩪𐩬|tr=ʾfrs¹n}}.

